package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setMurdersAtKarlovManor = StorySection(
    "mkm", "Murders at Karlov Manor",
    "A year after the Phyrexians fell, someone on Ravnica began killing the people who had failed it, and the only ones who could catch them were detectives the guilds had never needed before.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-the-set", "The Set",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-a-murder-mystery", "A Murder Mystery",
                    listOf(
                        "Murders at Karlov Manor came out on February 9, 2024, under the set code MKM. It was the ninety-ninth expansion in the game's history, and it went back once more to Ravnica, the city that covers a whole world. But it did not go back for the guilds. Every earlier Ravnica set had been built around the ten guilds and their rivalries. This one was built around a body on the floor of a rich woman's party, and the question of who put it there.",
                        "It was designed from the top down as a murder mystery. Mark Rosewater and Mark Gottlieb led the design, and the team started from the tropes of crime fiction rather than from the plane. The gala in the manor, the master detective, the gathering of suspects in one room, the disguises, the red herrings and the dramatic accusation all became cards. The set's tagline was simply Unravel the Mystery, and its expansion symbol is a dagger whose hilt is made from the old Return to Ravnica and Guilds of Ravnica symbols.",
                        "Because the story was a mystery, the cards were written to tell it in order. Murders at Karlov Manor carried far more Story Spotlight cards than usual, thirty of them, each showing one scene from the official story. A player who lined them up, from {Murder} at the party to {Assemble the Players} at the end, could follow the whole case from the first corpse to the culprit without reading a word of prose.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Laughter and conversation trailed off as the revelers gathered around Zegana's body. Three things quickly became clear: it was a murder, the killer was likely among them, and Teysa's party was definitely over.",
                        "Murder",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-in-the-omenpath-arc", "In the Omenpath Arc",
                    listOf(
                        "In the larger story, Murders at Karlov Manor is the third set of the Omenpath era, following The Lost Caverns of Ixalan and coming before Outlaws of [Thunder Junction](thunder-junction). It takes place roughly a year after the [Phyrexian invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse), on a Ravnica that had survived but had not healed. The guilds were broken in different ways, the people had stopped trusting them, and new doors called [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) were opening in the streets.",
                        "The story was written by Seanan McGuire and published as eleven episodes of Magic Story, the first in December 2023 and the last, Portents and Omens, on February 14, 2024. The main story is told in the Lore section as [Karlov Manor](karlov-manor), and this page goes deeper into the world around it. Kellan, the half-fae boy from Eldraine who had crossed to Ixalan through an Omenpath, comes through another one here and turns up as a junior inspector.",
                        "The set also hands the story on. Its last episode leaves the detective Alquist Proft pulling at a thread that leads straight to the dragon Niv-Mizzet and a secret plan to control the Omenpaths. Kellan leaves Ravnica soon after on a job that takes him to Thunder Junction, and the people trapped behind a door to [Duskmourn](duskmourn) would later need Proft too. What looked like a closed-room murder turned out to be the first chapter of a much larger argument about who owns the roads between worlds.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-the-first-play-booster", "The First Play Booster",
                    listOf(
                        "Murders at Karlov Manor also changed how Magic was sold. It was the first set to come in Play Boosters, fourteen playable cards plus an art or marketing card, a single pack meant both for drafting and for opening at home. Play Boosters replaced the old Draft Boosters and Set Boosters at once, and every Standard set since has followed. Collector Boosters remained for those chasing special treatments, and the bundle grew to nine packs.",
                        "The new pack shape changed the set itself. Because each pack held fewer commons, the design team cut the number of commons in the set from about a hundred to eighty and raised the uncommons to a hundred. The ten rare surveil lands were moved out of the rare slot and into the pack's wild slot, where they turned up far more often than ordinary rares, around one pack in six by most counts.",
                        "Some Play Boosters also held a card from The List, and for this set that included ten Special Guests, older cards reprinted with new art that fit a murder on Ravnica. {Ghostly Prison}, {Show and Tell}, {Tireless Tracker} and {Field of the Dead} were among them. The prerelease ran from February 2 to 8, and an extra annex game, Ravnica Clue Edition, followed two weeks after the set itself.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episodes 1 to 11, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Mark Rosewater, Getting Away with Murders at Karlov Manor, Making Magic"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-after-the-oil", "After the Oil",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-a-city-out-of-balance", "A City Out of Balance",
                    listOf(
                        "When the invasion came to Ravnica, the Invasion Tree drove up through the undercity and heaved whole districts into the air. It opened a road for the Golgari Swarm, already turned Phyrexian under a compleated Vraska, to pour into the city above. The other guilds rallied and fought back, most of them. Some of the Izzet and Simic, tempted by what Phyrexian oil might teach them, went over to the invaders and learned their mistake too late.",
                        "A year later the guilds were still looking for a new balance. Some were stretched thin, their ranks emptied by the dead, trying to meet the same demands with half the people. Some had turned inward to sort out their own wreckage and all but abandoned the jobs the Guildpact gave them. Some were using the confusion to chase their own ends. Relations between the guilds had cracked, and every crack was a place for something worse to grow.",
                        "Over all of it sat Niv-Mizzet, the Living Guildpact since the [War of the Spark](bolas-war-of-the-spark), who had declared himself the plane's supreme authority again. His seat, the Chamber of the Guildpact, had been destroyed twice, once in that war and once in the invasion. Money and goodwill to rebuild it kept running out, and the dragon refused to move, so guild dignitaries who wanted an audience still had to climb into its drafty, rickety skeleton to get one.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-five-guilds-strained", "Five Guilds Strained",
                    listOf(
                        "The Azorius Senate was drowning in law. Nothing on the books covered an interplanar invasion, and most of the Senate's strength went into rewriting the legal code. The courts clogged. To clear them the Azorius took only simple cases and quick arrests, and anything that needed real investigation was shelved or handed to outside detectives. Lavinia, acting guildmaster since the War of the Spark, had stopped resisting the job and did it with relentless care.",
                        "The Orzhov Syndicate saw opportunity where everyone else saw grief. It had fought hard in the defense, then moved fast once the enemy was gone, writing loans and contracts at ruinous rates and paying for showy public works to soften its name. Teysa Karlov had taken the guild back from Kaya during the invasion, but left its daily running to Tomik Vrona while she enjoyed her wealth. House Dimir staged the death of its entire leadership under cover of the fighting, and now even its own agents did not know who was in charge. In truth Lazav wore the faces of low-ranking agents and gave orders only through dead drops.",
                        "The Izzet League surprised everyone by doing its job. Ral Zarek strictly limited personal experiments and pointed the guild at reconstruction, and the public came round to it. Many inventors quit in protest and opened small labs of their own, which is why cheap detective gear was suddenly everywhere. The Boros Legion was in a crisis of faith. It had lost more soldiers than any guild, Aurelia took the losses hard, and the Legion cracked down harder on the Golgari, Simic, Rakdos and Gruul while filling its ranks with raw guildless recruits.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "After heavy casualties in the Phyrexian invasion, the Boros mage-priests trained as many rank-and-file soldiers as possible to wield the Legion's legendary battle magic.",
                        "Lightning Helix",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-five-guilds-adrift", "Five Guilds Adrift",
                    listOf(
                        "The Golgari Swarm had led the invasion into the city, and every other guild had denounced it. Those who escaped compleation were shunned, and many deserted. Vraska had vanished, and the faction leaders who remained stripped her of her title, then failed to agree on who should take it. The Gruul grabbed as much ruined ground as they could, all at once, and everybody assumed the clans had planned it together. They had not. It was coincidence, and now they held more territory than they could defend.",
                        "The Cult of Rakdos gave a grieving city what it wanted. Nightclubs and circus tents sprang up overnight, and parades wound through streets still choked with rubble, so often that the Boros kept wading in. Rakdos himself mostly stayed in his lair, and his top performers fought to outdo one another. The Selesnya Conclave shut its doors after the invasion and refused all help, then slowly opened again. Trostani had not been seen outside a hidden room in Vitu-Ghazi for months, and rumors spoke of schism and a lost bond with the Worldsoul.",
                        "The Simic Combine had come closest to Phyrexia of all the defenders. Many of its members underwent compleation willingly to study the oil, with disastrous results. Afterward Zegana tried to take the title of Prime Speaker back, blaming weak leadership, but Vannifar asked the other guilds for support and kept her seat. The Simic now offered treatments to people half compleated, and the irony was hard to miss. The oil research that disgraced them was what made the cures work.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "With their guild shunned for opening the doors to Phyrexia, some among the Golgari embraced their status as pariahs and took out their bitterness on all who crossed their paths.",
                        "Undercity Eliminator",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-new-places", "New Places",
                    listOf(
                        "Vitu-Ghazi, the old city tree of the Selesnya, had been replanted in a park to recover, and inside its dormant remains the Orzhov and Selesnya together founded the Museum of Ravnica. It was the only time the Conclave worked with anyone after the invasion. Its halls held a replica of the original Guildpact, lazotep pieces of the Eternalized god Oketra, and the shattered remains of [Gideon](who/gideon) Jura's Blackblade. Public galleries wound past private Conclave halls without ever meeting them.",
                        "Plaza Karlov was the newest and most expensive address on Ravnica, all elegant manors and paved avenues behind warded iron gates, and many saw it as a gross display of wealth. At its heart stood One Karlov Place, Teysa's manor, with glass spires and arched skybridges over a vast park, reached by a long winding drive designed so that visitors admired the whole estate before they reached the door. An invitation to one of her galas was proof that a person mattered.",
                        "Elsewhere the Hellbender nightclub, owned by Judith of the Rakdos and in Plaza West to the disgust of its older shops, offered nightly shows that blurred the line between watching and taking part. And where the Invasion Tree had lifted the undercity into daylight for the first time in centuries, a Risen Undercity had grown. Plants and creatures that had never seen the sun were adapting to it, and new species were still being found. One of those strange new growths would matter a great deal.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Murders at Karlov Manor, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Ravnica"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-the-agency", "The Agency",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-ezrims-truth-cult", "Ezrim's Truth Cult",
                    listOf(
                        "For most of Ravnica's history, being a detective was a small trade. The Boros and the Azorius claimed every criminal investigation, the guilds handled their own affairs in private, and independent agencies took lost rings and unfaithful partners. Then the guilds faltered, and ordinary Ravnicans began doing the work themselves, patrolling streets and mending pipes. Detection was one of those jobs. The guilds called the new detectives interlopers at first, and then, because they had no choice, they started hiring them.",
                        "An understanding settled in without anyone signing it. The guilds would not interfere with a detective's investigation, and in return the detectives would hand any real crime to the proper guild and never make arrests, hold prisoners or pass sentence. Detectives were often let into guild ground that no outsider could normally enter. Within a short time the number of agencies had grown tenfold, and the largest of them grew until it rivalled the guilds themselves.",
                        "That was the Ravnican Agency of Magicological Investigations. Its chief was Ezrim, a guildless archon who had once stood with the Azorius, found the paperwork tedious and his fellow archons insufferably smug, and walked away with a handful of followers. For centuries they were a quiet truth cult devoted to his twin ideals of clarity and truth, recruiting only those he judged worthy and charging just enough to keep helping people. When the invasion ended and the cases flooded in, the cult became an agency, with real fees and a business sense, though its heart was still his.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "In your search for a suspect, never forget that the most important person is the victim.",
                        "Chalk Outline",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-the-floating-eye", "The Floating Eye",
                    listOf(
                        "The Agency's headquarters hangs over Tenth District Plaza, ground that has always been neutral between the guilds. It looks like a vast floating eye. Artificial waterfalls pour from beneath it and wrap the plaza in permanent mist, so that the building seems to rise above the fog of every mystery. No stair or road reaches it. Visitors fly, or they ride up on one of the Agency's griffins. Inside, a huge atrium opens into a honeycomb of offices, laboratories and archives, all built wide enough for Ezrim, whose office is on the top floor.",
                        "The case archive at its heart keeps every closed or cold case the Agency has ever worked, the oldest no more than notes on scraps from the truth cult's early days, and the corridors leading there are warded so that nobody without an Agency badge can even find them. Evidence is kept in lockers only custodians may touch. Each sealed evidence capsule remembers everyone who has ever handled it. In the necropsy chambers, former Simic and Golgari read the dead.",
                        "Below Ezrim the Agency splits in two. Investigations has ten subdivisions, one for each of Ravnica's districts, handling homicide, crime, guild matters and civil cases, and homicides are almost always worked jointly with a guild. Support holds the analysts, the forensic mages and chirurgeons, the archivists and the guild liaisons. The Agency has no secret magic of its own, but every technique its people use works better for Ezrim's influence. Agents keep their notes on each case in projektors, and forensic thopters carry messages and help at the scene.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "It displays every fact, every suspect, and every scrap of evidence for every case the Agency oversees.",
                        "Panoptic Projektor",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-other-detectives", "Other Detectives",
                    listOf(
                        "The Agency was the largest, but far from the only one. Smaller agencies and freelancers lived on private work, finding lost things and catching cheats, and won clients by being cheaper or freer of paperwork. Not all of them honored the understanding with the guilds, and some withheld evidence or misled a guild outright to keep a case. Haazda Investigations worked the edges of the city beyond Boros reach, an offshoot of the Haazda volunteer watch, rougher than the Agency and quicker with their fists.",
                        "The Unguilded Eye was barely an agency at all, a loose web of freelancers who worked alone but shared what they knew when they had to. And then there were the amateurs, reporters and retired grandparents and nosy children who solved things with local knowledge and a keen eye, the cast of cards like {Meddling Youths}. The set's flavor text is full of minor detectives too, the partners of the Foundway Associates among them.",
                        "A whole trade grew up to supply them, much of it from Izzet inventors who had walked out on Ral's rules. There were toolkit bracers full of lenses, evidence tags that stopped anyone tampering with a scene, spectral lenses that read magical residue, darklamps tuned to glow on special dusting powders, sealed evidence capsules that held objects in stasis, and barrier wards to close off a scene. That last item would end up holding a guildmaster. {Magnifying Glass} and {Novice Inspector} are the trade in miniature.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "If the perfect evidence appears at your feet, your first task is to rule out misdirection.",
                        "Novice Inspector",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Murders at Karlov Manor, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Ravnican Agency of Magicological Investigations"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Ezrim"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-murder-at-the-gala", "Murder at the Gala",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-the-party", "The Party",
                    listOf(
                        "Teysa Karlov threw a celebration at her new manor, and every guild but the Golgari sent someone. She summoned Kaya home to stand for the Orzhov, though the two of them had fought over the guild, and in private she chided Kaya for failing to protect Ravnica during the invasion. Kaya answered that without the strike team she had been part of, the invasion would have won. Privately she was not sure that was true. Kaya was one of the few who had [kept her spark](sparks-lost), and among the guilds that made her a stranger.",
                        "Among the guests was a young inspector of the Agency named Kellan. He had come to Ravnica through an Omenpath, joined Ezrim's people hoping to learn about his father, and stopped the Gruul god Anzrag from rampaging across the Ninth District. A month later he was a guest of honor at the richest house in the city, with a purse of zinos for his trouble. Then Zegana, former Prime Speaker of the Simic, was killed in the middle of the party.",
                        "No guest was allowed to leave before Azorius lawmages questioned them in verity circles, and the Agency sent its best. Alquist Proft brushed past the guards, introduced himself without a trace of irony as the great Detective Proft, and found the culprit within minutes. She was Etrata, the Dimir's finest assassin, who had come to the party disguised as a member of the Selesnya. With help from Kaya and Kellan, she was taken. She insisted she had not done it. She simply could not remember.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Proft brushed past the guards and introduced himself as \"the great Detective Proft\" without a hint of irony, then identified the culprit within minutes.",
                        "Auspicious Arrival",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-teysa-falls", "Teysa Falls",
                    listOf(
                        "Three days later Kaya went back to Karlov Manor and found Teysa dead. Teysa's security was the best money could buy, and Etrata was locked in an Azorius cell. Beside the body was a note scrawled in Phyrexian, and the hand was Teysa's own. Whatever the note meant, it put the word Phyrexian next to the name of the richest woman on Ravnica, and in that city, a year after the oil, that was the most dangerous word there was.",
                        "Ezrim did not believe the case was closed. He asked Kaya, an outsider to every guild now and a ghost-assassin who could talk to the dead, to lead the investigation, and she took Kellan as her assistant. The boy was earnest and quick and badly wanted to prove himself. Kaya had just lost a friend and a rival in the same person. Neither of them knew yet that Teysa's ghost was not finished with Ravnica.",
                        "Meanwhile Proft had decided that his own arrest was wrong. Etrata had committed dozens of murders and remembered each one in perfect, loving detail, yet she had no memory of Zegana at all. That did not add up, and an inconsistency was the one thing Proft could not leave alone. Three days after catching her, he broke her out of a maximum security Azorius holding cell. He had never been a by the book investigator, but even he had not expected to do that.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Teysa's security was unrivaled. Etrata was locked up. Kaya was left with nothing but multiplying questions... and a dead friend.",
                        "Deadly Complication",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-the-rakdos-lead", "The Rakdos Lead",
                    listOf(
                        "So the case split in two. Kaya and Kellan went to see Judith, the star of the Cult of Rakdos and the only member of the cult at the party, who had been suspiciously absent when Zegana died. Judith pointed them toward the original text of the Guildpact, now kept in the museum inside Vitu-Ghazi. What it said about the Rakdos seemed to point at the demon guildmaster himself. Aurelia sent Agrus Kos, the old Wojek ghost, to work beside them, though he disliked stepping on the Agency's toes.",
                        "On the way back to headquarters a gang of thugs attacked Kaya and Kellan. The two of them won the fight, but before they could question anyone, the thugs swallowed pills that turned their bodies to moss. At headquarters there was news. Teysa's killer had been found, a guildless man with no memory of what he had done. It was Etrata's story again, in a stranger's body.",
                        "Then Aurelia arrived with Massacre Girl in tow. Massacre Girl had tried to kill the Boros guildmaster, cut down ten guards on the way, and only came back to herself after she had lost the fight. She remembered none of it. Aurelia had lost patience. She gave the Agency twenty-four hours to name the mastermind, and if they did not, the Boros Legion would go to war with the Cult of Rakdos, a war the guilds might not survive.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episode 1, Ghosts of Our Past, and Episode 2, Monsters We Became, by Seanan McGuire"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episode 3, Shadows of Regret, and Episode 4, Justice Before Mercy, by Seanan McGuire"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Kaya Cassir"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-the-two-trails", "The Two Trails",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-proft-and-etrata", "Proft and Etrata",
                    listOf(
                        "Proft and Etrata followed the trail backward, to the safehouse where she had slept the night before the party, the last place she remembered. On her bed was a fine yellow-gray powder. Proft thought it some kind of drug. On the way out, Massacre Girl struck. Judith had hired her to kill Proft as a message to the Agency to keep out of guild business, and she wounded him before she realized who he was traveling with. Massacre Girl respected Etrata, and she stood down.",
                        "They went to Kylox, an Izzet scientist and a friend of Proft's who might explain the powder. His lab had been ransacked. He was hiding in a secret passage and would talk only if they got him to Ezrim, and then Krenko's goblins snatched him. The pair tracked them to one of Krenko's safehouses, where the goblin boss wanted everything Kylox knew. Etrata dealt with the goblins, but before Kylox could say a word, a man burst through the window to kill Krenko and stabbed Kylox to death instead. When Etrata lifted the spell on him with her mind, he had no idea what he had done.",
                        "Their last hope for the powder was Izoni of the Golgari, down in the undercity of the Sixth Precinct. She told them it came from a living thing that was not native to Ravnica. Then Proft noticed someone listening, gave chase and was struck down by an unseen force. He woke inside his own mind palace, which he had not summoned, beside a stranger in a hooded cloak. It was [Jace Beleren](who/jace), who wanted to understand how Proft could project his thoughts into reality. Jace took one of the files from Proft's shelves and let him go.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Proft had never been a by-the-book investigator, but even he never expected to find himself breaking a notorious assassin out of a maximum-security Azorius holding cell.",
                        "Unauthorized Exit",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-twenty-four-hours", "Twenty-Four Hours",
                    listOf(
                        "Back at the Agency, Kaya and Kellan spread their evidence out in his cramped office and decided that Rakdos had been set up. He was no real suspect. Then the alarms went off. Anzrag, the Gruul god Kellan had brought in, had broken out of his evidence capsule and was tearing through headquarters. Kaya drew him off while Kellan fetched the capsule, Ezrim faced the god down, and together they sealed Anzrag away again.",
                        "Agrus Kos had gone on alone to the basement of Rix Maadi, sure that Rakdos was being framed. He was right, and it cost him. Judith had sent the Agency to the Guildpact on purpose, hoping the guilds would destroy Rakdos and leave her the cult. When Kos came close to the truth, she trapped his ghost in a crystal skull. Aurelia came to Ezrim and told him that Kos was missing and that she would not wait any longer.",
                        "Kaya stepped away from the shouting and found a courier waiting with a message. Someone wanted her at Karlov Cathedral, where Teysa's funeral was being prepared. It was Proft, with Etrata at his side. Kaya gave him the three things he had not had, the Phyrexian note by Teysa's body, the attempt on Aurelia, and the thugs who had turned to moss. Proft lit up like a man handed an unexpected gift. He knew who was responsible, and he could prove it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "After the attempt on Aurelia's life, she gave the Agency an ultimatum: solve the case in twenty-four hours, or the Boros Legion would declare war on the Cult of Rakdos.",
                        "Demand Answers",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episode 5, Chains of Expectation, and Episode 6, Explosions of Genius, by Seanan McGuire"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episode 7, Rot Before Recovery, and Episode 8, Gods of Chaos, by Seanan McGuire"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Alquist Proft"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Agrus Kos"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-the-reveal", "The Reveal",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-in-this-very-room", "In This Very Room",
                    listOf(
                        "Proft had Kaya bring the most important people in the city to one place, Trostani's private study deep inside Vitu-Ghazi. Krenko came, and Aurelia, and Lavinia and Vannifar and Ral, and Judith and Izoni and Tolsimir Wolfblood with them. Proft told them the killer was in the room and that he would question them one by one. He took Kaya into a side room first, and the two of them sat in a long and uncomfortable silence.",
                        "Then a flower pushed up through the floor. As it swelled to release its pollen, Kaya cut the stem, and Proft sealed it in an Agency evidence capsule before it could wither away. The questioning had been bait. By claiming he had nearly solved the case, Proft had made himself a target, and he had chosen Kaya for his interview because a planeswalker who killed him could simply leave the plane afterward. It would have been the perfect crime, and the killer could not resist it.",
                        "He brought the flower out and laid out the reasoning. Everyone in the case had lost their memory just before their crime and found it again just after. The powder did not grow wild on Ravnica, but it could have been cultivated, and it showed none of the marks of Phyrexian work. It could not work twice on the same person, or the killer would have sent Etrata after Proft and proved her guilt at a stroke. It was a strong, careful plant with built in limits, the work of someone with power over nature and deep respect for it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "All will soon be made clear, but I can give you this: our killer is here with us, in this very room.",
                        "Assemble the Players",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-oba", "Oba",
                    listOf(
                        "That pointed at the Selesnya, and it explained the delivery. The roots of Vitu-Ghazi ran under the whole city, so a skilled nature mage could raise a flower anywhere, let it breathe on its victim and then let it wither without a trace. Tolsimir Wolfblood stepped forward and confessed to the murders and to sending the thugs. Proft refused his confession. The killer was Trostani, and more exactly Oba, the dryad of Life, who had moved to the center of the three when the city tree was replanted.",
                        "During the invasion Oba had poured her own strength into Vitu-Ghazi, without her sisters knowing, to keep Realmbreaker from corrupting it. Her senses spread through the plane. She felt everyone who died or was compleated, and she saw the Ravnicans who used the invasion for their own gain. She chose them as her victims. Zegana had experimented with the oil. Teysa had been a double agent who looked like a collaborator. Krenko had profited. Others had died quietly before Zegana, people nobody noticed, until a famous murder brought a famous detective.",
                        "When Aurelia moved to arrest her, Oba knocked her sisters unconscious, took the whole of Trostani's power and woke Vitu-Ghazi against everyone in the room. Branches seized Ezrim. Kellan was hurled up through a gap in the ceiling and, falling, found that the fae magic in his sword hilts let him fly. Etrata threw herself in front of the Gruul centaur Yarus and took a branch through the chest. She played dead until her moment came, then helped Kaya and the ghost of Teysa trap Oba in an Agency barrier ward until her sisters woke and put her to sleep.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Hoarding supplies, profiting from death and destruction, colluding with the invaders—every one of them betrayed Ravnica in its time of greatest need. They all deserved to die. And I'm not done yet!",
                        "Worldsoul's Rage",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-loose-threads", "Loose Threads",
                    listOf(
                        "In the confusion Judith fled, and Massacre Girl, who had learned that Judith left her to the Boros, went after her with a knife. Nobody has seen Judith since, and Agrus Kos was left in his crystal skull. Oba's sisters went into meditation to bring her back to harmony, leaving Emmara Tandris to lead the Selesnya, and Lavinia searched the Guildpact for a way to try a guildmaster and found almost nothing. Kaya wished she had killed Oba in the fight. Teysa's ghost promised instead to sue the Conclave until it wished she had.",
                        "At the funeral Teysa's spirit took back the Orzhov and advised Kaya to leave Ravnica for a while so the succession would stay clear, and Kaya went. Etrata told Proft she would stay on as his aide. Her guild was weak, she said, and Lazav was dead. Proft suspected the second part was a lie. Kellan went back to his desk at the Agency, and later took a job on Niv-Mizzet's relay project that carried him to Thunder Junction in search of his father.",
                        "One thread was left. In the final episode, Portents and Omens, Proft cracked the cryptex Kylox had left behind and found that the scientist had been working for Niv-Mizzet on something called the Omenpath Project. The Firemind had seen the new paths as a threat and meant to control their opening and closing, and make Ravnica the center of the Multiverse. Proft and Etrata went to see the dragon. Proft told him he knew, and offered his skills, for a consulting fee.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episode 9, Beauty in Destruction, and Episode 10, Roots of Decay, by Seanan McGuire"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episode 11, Portents and Omens, by Seanan McGuire"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Trostani"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Etrata"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-the-detectives", "The Detectives",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-alquist-proft", "Alquist Proft",
                    listOf(
                        "Alquist Proft is Ravnica's great detective, and he would tell you so. He is a human in a long azure coat, his dark hair gone gray at the temples, a former lawmage of the Azorius Senate who joined Ezrim's Agency after the invasion. Before that he spent time in the undercity learning how the Golgari lived, because he wanted to understand the largest guild in the city. He has never trained his body at all. Everything he has is in his head, and he notices everything.",
                        "He has a perfect memory and can raise an illusion of any place he has ever seen, rebuilding a crime scene around himself to walk through it again, though it exhausts him. He can open simple locks with a thought. He can step inside his own mind and walk through it as a palace of shelves and files, the place [Jace](who/jace) broke into during the case. Hunger for the truth drives him, and it often puts him on the wrong side of the law, which is how a man who arrested an assassin ended up breaking her out.",
                        "His card, {Alquist Proft, Master Sleuth}, turns Clues into cards and life, and {Proft's Eidetic Memory} rewards a player for drawing more than one card a turn. {Reenact the Crime} shows him rebuilding Kylox's ruined workshop from memory. By the end of the set he had a partner, a reputation that reached a dragon, and a contract with Niv-Mizzet to help map and secure every Omenpath on Ravnica.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "At Proft's command, an ethereal model of the ransacked workshop sprang into being, every detail perfectly recreated. He'd missed something—he knew it—but what?",
                        "Reenact the Crime",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-etrata-and-kaya", "Etrata and Kaya",
                    listOf(
                        "Etrata is a vampire assassin, once a champion of House Dimir, and she plays the faithful companion to Proft's genius in this story, though nobody would call her faithful. She is cold, fashionable and frighteningly good at her work, with silent footsteps and a gaze that can freeze a victim with fear. She cares little for Ravnica and only somewhat for her guild. She never kills for free, and she remembers every kill. During the invasion she was one of Teysa's contacts in the resistance.",
                        "When the Dimir seemed to vanish she sold her skills to anyone who could pay, and grew bored. Being framed for a murder she could not remember gave her the most interesting job she had had in a year. {Etrata, Deadly Fugitive} hides creatures face down and steals the top of an opponent's library. Kaya, the ghost-assassin of Tolvada, was the investigation's other lead. She could pass through solid matter and speak to the dead, and it was her phasing that let her survive Oba's branches in Vitu-Ghazi.",
                        "Kaya's place on Ravnica had become strange. She had once been guildmaster of the Orzhov and lost it to Teysa, she had kept her spark when most of her old friends lost theirs, and many Ravnicans blamed planeswalkers for both wars that had wrecked their city. {Kaya, Spirits' Justice} exiles and copies creatures and makes flying spirits, a fitting shape for someone who ended the case with a ghost on one side and an assassin on the other.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Etrata had committed dozens of murders, all of which she recalled in perfect, loving detail. But no matter how hard she tried, she couldn't remember killing Zegana.",
                        "It Doesn't Add Up",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-kellan-and-ezrim", "Kellan and Ezrim",
                    listOf(
                        "Kellan is half human and half fae, the son of Alyse of Orrinshire and the planeswalker Oko, and he has no spark. After [ending the Wicked Slumber](wilds-of-eldraine) on Eldraine and following an Omenpath to Ixalan, he came through another to Ravnica and joined the Agency, hoping it could help him find his father. His office was a half-walled corner alcove off the main floor with a desk, a rickety file cabinet and a small brass projektor whose stylus seemed to be one of Ezrim's feathers.",
                        "He is wiry and small for his age, honest perhaps to the point of being unable to lie, and he carries the two basket hilts the fae lord Talion gave him, which light with blades of ethereal thorn. At the climax he learned that the same fae magic lets him fly. {Kellan, Inquisitive Prodigy} has flying and investigates, and Ezrim told him, at the end of all this, where Oko had gone.",
                        "Ezrim himself is enormous, stern and humorless, a brilliant and uncompromising mind bound to no guild, who carries a great lance and wears a long elaborate cape. His mount looks like a cross between an ox and a lion with long talons, horns and two pairs of feathered wings. Rider and mount are separate beings who act as one and are never more than an arm's reach apart. {Ezrim, Agency Chief} investigates twice as he arrives.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "When you said I could learn a lot from a corpse, I have to admit I wasn't expecting it to lecture me on forensic procedure.",
                        "Curious Cadaver",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episodes 1 to 11, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Alquist Proft"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Kellan"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-the-suspects", "The Suspects",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-the-usual-suspects", "The Usual Suspects",
                    listOf(
                        "The set's key art lined up its suspects like a board game, and the official list was Massacre Girl, Etrata, Izoni, Krenko, Judith, Kylox, Tolsimir Wolfblood, Rakdos and Aurelia. Every one of them had a motive somebody could believe. Judith had the best one. She did eight shows a week while the demon got the praise, she owned the Hellbender, and she wanted the cult for herself. She hired Massacre Girl to kill Proft and framed her own guildmaster. {Judith, Carnage Connoisseur} rewards every spell with deathtouch and lifelink or a little exploding imp.",
                        "Massacre Girl, a killer once named among the Azorius Ten Most Wanted, was both a hired blade and a victim in this story, sent against Aurelia by the pollen. {Massacre Girl, Known Killer} gives your creatures wither and draws a card whenever an enemy creature shrivels away. Krenko, the goblin crime boss of Tin Street, had done well out of the invasion and was on Oba's list for it. He survived, and {Krenko, Baron of Tin Street} still makes goblins out of broken artifacts.",
                        "Rakdos himself was never really a suspect, only a convenient one. {Rakdos, Patron of Chaos} gives an opponent the choice of sacrificing two permanents or letting you draw two cards, and his flavor text applauds a performance at last worthy of him. Aurelia, who survived her assassin and nearly started a war, became {Aurelia, the Law Above}, drawing cards whenever three or more creatures attack.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "'Did I do it?' You'll have to be more specific.",
                        "Massacre Girl, Known Killer",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-guildmasters-and-ghosts", "Guildmasters and Ghosts",
                    listOf(
                        "Teysa Karlov, the victim, was by far the wealthiest person on Ravnica, and as a ghost she was not done. {Teysa, Opulent Oligarch} investigates whenever opponents lose life and turns each spent Clue into a flying spirit. Her steward Tomik Vrona ran the guild while she spent its money, and the prerelease promo {Tomik, Wielder of Law} shows him. Agrus Kos, the old Wojek officer of the first Ravnica novels, returned as {Agrus Kos, Spirit of Justice}, a ghost detective who suspects creatures and then exiles them.",
                        "Trostani, the three dryads Oba, Ses and Cim, were the hidden heart of the case, and their card {Trostani, Three Whispers} hands out deathtouch, vigilance and double strike, one gift for each sister. Tolsimir Wolfblood, who tried to take the blame for them, came with his wolf Voja as {Tolsimir, Midnight's Light}. Izoni of the Golgari, who told Proft where the powder came from, is {Izoni, Center of the Web}, and Kylox, whose death left the last thread, is {Kylox, Visionary Inventor}.",
                        "Some legends never stood in the room at all. Lazav, secretly running the Dimir under borrowed faces, is {Lazav, Wearer of Faces}. Vannifar, clinging to the Simic, is {Vannifar, Evolved Enigma}. Yarus, the Gruul centaur Etrata saved, is {Yarus, Roar of the Old Gods}. And Anzrag, the mole god who escaped twice, is {Anzrag, the Quake-Mole}, a creature that attacks again whenever it is blocked. Delney, a streetwise lookout who sold information to anyone, had a card of her own as well.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I don't make my living hiding the truth, detective. I shout it from the stage.",
                        "Judith, Carnage Connoisseur",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Story", "Murders at Karlov Manor, Episodes 1 to 11, by Seanan McGuire, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Judith"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Massacre Girl"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-the-mechanics", "The Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-disguise-and-cloak", "Disguise and Cloak",
                    listOf(
                        "A murder mystery needs people who are not what they seem, so the set brought back the oldest trick in Magic's book, the face-down creature. Disguise works like the old morph from Onslaught and Khans of Tarkir. You may cast a card with disguise face down for three generic mana as a nameless 2/2, and later pay its disguise cost to turn it face up whenever you like. The difference is a shield. A disguised creature has ward of two mana, so an opponent who wants to kill your mystery creature has to pay for the privilege.",
                        "Cloak is the matching action, the cousin of manifest. To cloak a card is to put it onto the battlefield face down as a 2/2 with the same ward, and if the card is a creature you may turn it face up for its mana cost. {Cryptic Coat} cloaks the top card of your library, makes it bigger and unblockable, and can return to your hand to do it again, which made it a fixture of Standard. {Vannifar, Evolved Enigma} cloaks a card from your hand every combat.",
                        "A small helper card called A Mysterious Creature came with the set to stand in for face-down cards. {Hunted Bonebrute}, a nod to the Hunted cycle of the original Ravnica, has disguise too. In Limited the face-down game ran through three archetypes, big disguise in red and green, go-wide disguise in green and white, and small disguise creatures in white and black, and the red green legend {Yarus, Roar of the Old Gods} brings dying face-down creatures straight back up.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "But then he peeled off the mask, revealing a sight that made Groja's blood run cold—Brulu was actually Vogos in disguise!",
                        "Crime Novelist",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-suspect-and-evidence", "Suspect and Evidence",
                    listOf(
                        "Suspect is a keyword action. When a creature is suspected, every eye in the city is on it, and it gains menace and loses the ability to block. It stays suspected until something clears it. You can suspect your own creature to make it a harder attacker, or an opponent's to strip away a blocker, and some cards punish creatures that are already under suspicion. {Agrus Kos, Spirit of Justice} suspects a creature when it enters or attacks, and exiles it if it was already a suspect. {Case of the Stashed Skeleton} makes a skeleton that starts out suspected.",
                        "Collect evidence is a cost. To collect evidence eight, for example, you exile cards from your graveyard whose mana values add up to eight or more. Some cards make it optional for a bigger effect, like {Analyze the Pollen}, which finds a land normally but any creature or land if you collected evidence. Others demand it outright. {Urgent Necropsy} destroys up to one artifact, creature, enchantment and planeswalker, but you must exile evidence equal to the total mana value of what it hits.",
                        "Evidence asked players to treat their graveyard as a case file. The five uncommon split cards were built for it. Each pairs two guilds that share a color, and the right half is always a sorcery costing six, which makes those cards heavy evidence once they hit the yard. Fuss and Bother is Boros and Azorius, Flotsam and Jetsam is Simic and Dimir, Push and Pull is Orzhov and Rakdos, Hustle and Bustle is Izzet and Gruul, and Cease and Desist is Golgari and Selesnya.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-cases-and-clues", "Cases and Clues",
                    listOf(
                        "The set added a new enchantment type, the Case. Every Case gives you something while it is open, sets you a goal under the heading To solve, and grants a reward once solved. The check comes at the beginning of your end step. If you meet the goal then, the Case is marked solved for good. {Case of the Locked Hothouse} lets you play an extra land each turn, and once you control seven lands you may play lands and cast creatures and enchantments from the top of your library. {Case of the Ransacked Lab} makes spells cheaper and, once solved, draws a card for every spell.",
                        "Investigate returned as the set's backbone, because detectives look for clues. To investigate is to create a Clue token, an artifact you can pay two mana and sacrifice to draw a card. The set had a minor theme around drawing your second card each turn, which Clues feed nicely, and the Detective creature type, first printed in the Doctor Who Commander decks months before, came into its own with typal payoffs.",
                        "The set also brought back basic landcycling for fixing, and it gave white and blue a Detectives deck, blue and black a Clue control deck, black and red suspect aggression, blue and red artifact sacrifice, black and green graveyard recursion, red and white an attack-with-three theme in the spirit of the old battalion keyword, and green and blue collect evidence. It introduced bloodstain, impostor and unlock counters along the way.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Lies fester and flourish in shadow. Our job is to bathe them in light.",
                        "No More Lies",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Matt Tabak, Murders at Karlov Manor Mechanics, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-cards-worth-knowing", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-the-legends", "The Legends",
                    listOf(
                        "The set is built on a double cycle of mythics, one for each guild, and most of them are the story's leading players. {Alquist Proft, Master Sleuth} stands for the Azorius, {Etrata, Deadly Fugitive} for the Dimir, {Rakdos, Patron of Chaos} for the Rakdos, {Anzrag, the Quake-Mole} for the Gruul, {Trostani, Three Whispers} for the Selesnya, {Kaya, Spirits' Justice} for the Orzhov, {Agrus Kos, Spirit of Justice} for the Boros and {Vannifar, Evolved Enigma} for the Simic. The Izzet and Golgari slots went to two spells instead, {Kylox's Voltstrider} and {Urgent Necropsy}.",
                        "Under them sits a rare cycle of two-color legends, ten more commanders. Ezrim, Lazav, Judith, Yarus, Tolsimir, Teysa, Kylox, Izoni, Aurelia and Kellan each lead a guild pair. Teysa makes Clues from lost life, Lazav copies what he exiles, and Kellan breaks artifacts and draws. Three more legends appeared only as prerelease promos, Tomik, the wolf {Voja, Jaws of the Conclave} and {Melek, Reforged Researcher}, a Weird detective, and none of the three is legal in Standard or Limited.",
                        "Beside the legends sit the cards people reach for. {Leyline of the Guildpact} makes every nonland permanent you control every color and every land every basic type, a nod to the leylines of the old Guildpact set. {Doppelgang} copies permanents by the handful. {Worldsoul's Rage} burns anything and drops lands into play. {Deduce} is a cheap cantrip that also investigates. {Delney, Streetwise Lookout} doubles the triggers of small creatures, and {Krenko's Buzzcrusher} is a flying insect that destroys nonbasic lands.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Suspect spotted at the Tin Street Market, Yescu Street entrance. And the Oak Street entrance. And somehow also in Lobar Alley!",
                        "Doppelgang",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-lands-and-weapons", "Lands and Weapons",
                    listOf(
                        "The ten surveil lands were the set's most valued rares. Each has two basic land types, enters tapped and lets you surveil one as it arrives. {Meticulous Archive} is white and blue, {Undercity Sewers} blue and black, {Raucous Theater} black and red, {Commercial District} red and green, {Lush Portico} green and white, {Shadowy Backstreet} white and black, {Thundering Falls} blue and red, {Underground Mortuary} black and green, {Elegant Parlor} red and white and {Hedge Maze} green and blue. Their names read like a tour of Ravnica's districts.",
                        "Set beside them, five uncommon equipment cards came straight out of the board game Clue. {Wrench}, {Candlestick}, {Lead Pipe}, {Knife} and {Rope} are each a weapon you can equip and also a Clue you can sacrifice for a card. Five of the six classic weapons were there. The revolver was left out. The same game sat behind the rare Case cycle, five open cases for five colors, each with a long, slow payoff.",
                        "Other cards nodded to the plane's past. {Hunted Bonebrute} recalls the Hunted creatures of the first Ravnica set, which were powerful but gave their opponent tokens, and here the Bonebrute gives the opponent two dogs to hunt it. The guild split cards echo the split cards that have been a Ravnica tradition since Dissension. And the full-art Impossible basic lands, five puzzles of bent perspective, each asked the viewer to work out how the picture could be true.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-special-treatments", "Special Treatments",
                    listOf(
                        "The set had more alternate looks than most. The Magnified showcase gave the Story Spotlight cards a showcase frame of their own. The Ravnica City frame wrapped a small group of legends in the city's spires, and in Collector Boosters those same cards appeared as serialized double rainbow foils, two hundred and fifty English copies of each. The borderless treatment went to the surveil lands and to Kellan and Kaya.",
                        "The Dossier showcase dressed cards up as Agency case files, and some of those were printed again with invisible ink, a foil that hides surprises on the card until a player goes looking. Those were found only in Collector Boosters. Several invisible ink cards carried new flavor text of their own, among them {Agrus Kos, Spirit of Justice} and {Delney, Streetwise Lookout}, both with lines that point at Judith.",
                        "The promos went further. The buy-a-box promo was an alternate art Wojek Investigator, the bundle promo an alternate art Axebane Ferox, and the seasonal dark frame promos included {Long Goodbye} and {Lightning Helix}, whose new flavor text explains that the Boros now taught their battle magic to rank and file soldiers. There was even a puzzle hunt, a mystery spread across the cards, the products and the web, with twelve smaller cases for players to crack on the way.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "As the days grew longer, so did the list of missing persons.",
                        "Long Goodbye",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Mike Turian and Adam Styborski, Collecting Murders at Karlov Manor, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Murders at Karlov Manor"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "mkm-commander-and-clue", "Commander and Clue",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-four-cases", "Four Cases",
                    listOf(
                        "Four Commander decks came out with the set, led by Gavin Verhey, each with its own new detective at the head. Between them they added forty-five new cards to Magic, and one card, {Ransom Note}, appeared in all four with four different pieces of art. It is a one-mana Clue that surveils as it enters and, when sacrificed, can cloak a card, goad a creature or simply draw. Several old reprints received new art to fit Ravnica as well.",
                        "Deadly Disguise is red, green and white, led by {Kaust, Eyes of the Glade}, a dryad detective who turns attacking face-down creatures face up and draws when they connect. It is a deck of big surprises, with new cards such as {Printlifter Ooze}, {Duskana, the Rage Mother} and {Tesak, Judith's Hellhound}. Revenant Recon is blue and black, led by the vampire {Mirko, Obsessive Theorist}, who grows whenever you surveil and pulls smaller creatures back from the graveyard each turn.",
                        "Deep Clue Sea is green, white and blue, led by {Morska, Undersea Sleuth}, a vedalken fish detective who investigates every upkeep and grows when you draw your second card. The deck's other face is {Sophia, Dogged Detective}, who arrives with Tiny, a legendary dog detective. Blame Game is red and white, led by {Nelly Borca, Impulsive Accuser}, who suspects a creature each time she attacks, then goads every suspect on the table, and draws cards when the fights she starts reach someone else.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "If you think you're being watched, you're probably right.",
                        "Kaust, Eyes of the Glade",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mkm-ravnica-clue-edition", "Ravnica Clue Edition",
                    listOf(
                        "Two weeks after the main set, on February 23, 2024, came Ravnica Clue Edition, known outside North America as Ravnica Cluedo Edition. It was a boxed game in the Universes Beyond line, made for the seventy-fifth anniversary of Clue, and it was not a reskin of the board game. Magic's designers built a new way to play in which three or four people each shuffle two half decks together and play Magic while trying to work out a hidden murder.",
                        "Each box held eight boosters drawn from twenty possible half decks, two for each guild, plus twenty-one evidence cards for the suspects, rooms and weapons of Clue reimagined for Ravnica, hidden information screens, an evidence notepad and a case file envelope. Each booster carried a new rare, and the rest were reprints from older Ravnica sets, with a shock land on top of every box. The launch party promo was a new legend, Lavinia, Foil to Conspiracy.",
                        "Taken together, the Commander decks, the Clue game, the puzzle hunt and a separate tabletop mystery published by Beadle and Grimm's, The Case of the Three Blade Knife, wrapped the whole season in the same mystery. For a few weeks in early 2024 almost every product on the shelf was asking the same question as Proft in Trostani's study. Who did it, and how, and can you prove it.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Murders at Karlov Manor Commander"),
                StorySource("Set", "Ravnica Clue Edition"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Murders at Karlov Manor Commander decks"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Ravnica Clue Edition"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
